course: everyday
lesson: 437
title: When not to keep a friend's secret
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Aisha's teacher drew two boxes on the board: SURPRISES and SECRETS. A surprise is kept for a short time and makes people happy when it is told — a birthday present, a party. A secret that makes you feel worried, scared or sick inside is a secret to tell. Kai's friend told him he liked a girl in class — «don't tell anyone!» — and Kai kept that one forever. That is a good secret: private, and nobody is hurt. Aisha's class made posters, and one boy told his teacher about something that had worried him for months.
explain: Lily's friend told her she was being hit at home and made her promise not to tell. Lily kept the promise for a week and felt sick every day. Then she told her teacher. Her friend was angry at first, but got help, and months later thanked her. Breaking a promise like that is not betrayal; it is help. Maya's friend, upset after a row, said she would run away that night and asked Maya to say nothing. Maya told her mum, who rang the friend's mum. The friend was cross for a day, then the family talked and sorted things out.
explain: Some secrets come wrapped in threats. Jack's mate said a man online had asked him for photos of himself and told him to keep it secret. A grown-up who asks a child to hide things from parents is a danger sign. Jack told his mum; she called his mate's parents and the police, and the man was found and stopped. Sam's big cousin was smoking behind the garage: «Grass me up and you're dead» — «grass» means tell on. Then he offered Sam one. Sam said no and told his uncle. Threats are a sign the secret is not okay, and the threats stopped.
explain: Others come wrapped in friendship. «I'll be your friend forever if you don't tell — but if you do, I'll never speak to you again,» said Noah's friend, who was bullying a younger kid. Noah told a teacher anyway. The friend didn't speak to him for a month, then came back and said sorry. Finn's friend smashed a school window on purpose and said «If you tell, they'll think you did it too.» Finn told his dad, who went with him to school, and the head believed him because he came forward. People who come forward early are usually believed.
explain: Ruby's friend in Glasgow had been hurting herself on purpose and begged, «Promise me, Rubes.» Ruby was terrified. She told the school nurse, and her friend got help from people who knew how. Telling was the most loving thing she could do. And Emma, whose friend kept taking money from her mum's purse, said: «I won't tell this time, but if it happens again, I'm telling.» The friend stopped. You can give a friend one chance to stop a small wrong — but if it keeps happening, tell.
fact: A surprise is kept for a short time and makes people happy when told; a secret that makes you worried, scared or sick should be told.
fact: Good secrets, like surprises, crushes and private feelings, should be kept.
fact: Secrets about someone being hurt, in danger, or hurting themselves must be told to a trusted grown-up.
fact: Telling a grown-up about a friend in danger is help, not betrayal, even if the friend is angry at first.
fact: A grown-up who asks a child to keep secrets from parents, especially online, is a danger sign: always tell.
fact: A threat like «tell and you're dead» is a sign the secret is not okay.
fact: «I'll never be your friend again if you tell» is pressure, and a friendship that depends on hiding bullying is not worth much.
fact: People who come forward early are usually believed; hiding is what makes you look guilty.
fact: For a small wrong, you can give a friend one chance to stop; if it keeps happening, tell.
fact: Trusted grown-ups include parents, teachers, school nurses and other family adults.
fact: When someone says they will run away, it is a «don't tell» that does not count, because they could get hurt.
fact: To «grass» or «grass up» (UK) means to tell on someone.
exercise: What did Lily's friend tell her? | She was being hit at home | She had a big crush on a boy in their class | She lost her homework again | She was moving house in the summer
exercise: How did Lily feel while she kept the secret? | Sick every day | Proud and happy to be trusted | Bored with it | Nothing at all
exercise: What happened after Lily told her teacher? | Her friend got help and later thanked her | Her friend never spoke to her again, not even once | Nobody did anything about it | Lily got in trouble
exercise: What did the man online ask Jack's mate to do? | Send photos of himself and keep it secret | Play an online game with him most nights | Buy him a present | Tell his parents everything about their chats
exercise: What happened after Jack told his mum? | The police found and stopped the man | The man sent even more messages to the boy | Jack's mate was punished by the school | Nothing happened, nobody believed Jack
exercise: What did Maya's friend plan to do that night? | Run away from home | Go to a party | Sleep at Maya's | Run a race
exercise: What did Sam's cousin say to keep him quiet? | «Grass me up and you're dead» | «I'll give you my bike if you keep quiet» | «It's only a sweet, nobody cares» | «Mum knows already, so it's fine»
exercise: Why should Emma tell if it happens again? | Taking money keeps happening and is a real wrong | Her friend asked her to tell someone about it | The money in the purse was really Emma's own pocket money | It is her mum's purse
exercise: Which secret did Kai keep forever? | His friend's crush on a girl | That his friend stole money from a shop | That his friend was hurt | That his friend smoked
exercise: According to Aisha's teacher, which should be kept? | A surprise that makes people happy when told | A secret that makes you feel scared | A secret about someone being hurt at home by a grown-up | A secret from a grown-up online
exercise: What did Noah's friend threaten? | Never to speak to him again | To hit him after school | To tell his mum a lie | To break his bike
exercise: What happened to Noah's friendship after he told? | The friend came back after a month and said sorry | The friend never spoke to him again, even years later | Noah was punished too | Noah changed school
exercise: Who did Ruby tell about her friend hurting herself? | The school nurse | Nobody at all | The whole class | Her friend's little brother
exercise: Why did the head believe Finn about the window? | He came forward early | He had a photo on his phone | His dad was a teacher | He said it twice
exercise: What did Finn's friend say to keep him quiet? | «If you tell, they'll think you did it too» | «I'll give you my phone if you keep quiet» | «It was an accident» | «Nobody will care»
exercise: What makes a secret one you should tell? | It makes you feel worried, scared or sick | It is about a birthday present you are buying for Mum | It is very old | It is about someone's crush
test: Friend: «promise u won't tell, I'm running away tonight». What should you do? | Tell a grown-up, because she could get hurt | Keep the promise no matter what happens | Help her pack | Go with her so she isn't alone
test: Online «friend», a grown-up: «don't tell your parents about our chats». What is this? | A danger sign — tell a grown-up at once | A normal friendly rule for online chats | A game everyone plays | A secret you should keep
test: Big cousin: «grass me up and you're dead». What does that threat show? | The secret is not okay, so tell a grown-up | He's just joking around, like big cousins do | The secret is fine to keep | Sam should join in
test: Friend: «Promise me, Rubes» — she's hurting herself. Is telling a betrayal? | No, it's the most loving thing to do | Yes, a promise is a promise, whatever it's about | Only if she's your very best friend at school | Yes, she'll never ever forgive you for it
test: «I've got a crush on Ellie, don't tell anyone!!» What should you do? | Keep it — it's a good, private secret | Tell the whole class | Tell Ellie straight away | Tell a teacher at once
test: What would happen if Finn kept quiet and someone saw him near the window later? | He might look guilty for hiding it | He would be believed even more | Nobody could ever suspect him of anything | The window would be forgotten
test: Mum's planning a surprise party for Dad on Saturday. Keep it or tell? | Keep it — it's a happy surprise for a short time | Tell Dad at once, because secrets are always wrong | Tell a teacher | Tell the police
test: Friend took money from her mum's purse once. What's a fair first step? | Give her one chance to stop, and tell if it happens again | Take some money too | Tell the whole school about it in assembly | Never mention it ever
test: Lily's friend was angry when Lily told. What happened months later? | She thanked Lily | She still hated Lily | She moved away forever | She told on Lily too
test: Kid: «real friends never tell, whatever». What's true? | Real friends tell when someone could get hurt | Real friends keep every secret, whatever it is | Real friends never talk to teachers | Telling always ends friendships
test: In a cartoon, a kid keeps a dangerous secret alone and fixes it all himself. What's wiser in real life? | Tell a trusted grown-up who can help | Handle it alone, just like the cartoon kid | Tell only other kids in your class | Wait until you're older and can sort it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between a surprise and a secret which ought to be disclosed? | A surprise is temporary and occasions pleasure; a secret occasioning fear or distress ought to be disclosed | All secrets are to be preserved without exception, whatever their content | All surprises are to be disclosed at once | No distinction exists between them, both being forms of concealment
exam: Identify the principal indication of danger in an adult's request, made online, that a child conceal communications from his parents. | The request for secrecy from parents itself | The use of a computer for the communication | The friendly manner of the adult | The time of day of the communication
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning disclosure to a trusted adult that a friend is being harmed. | It constitutes assistance and not betrayal | It constitutes a betrayal of friendship | It is permissible only with the friend's express consent | It is of no benefit to the friend
exam: It is given that a pupil, threatened with false blame, reports at once an act of damage committed by another. Determine the consequence most commonly reported. | The pupil is believed, having come forward early | The pupil is presumed to be guilty of the damage | The pupil is punished for reporting | The report is disregarded
